{"step_index":279,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:14:38Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-17T16:14:38Z\nCompleted At: 2026-08-17T16:14:38Z\nFile Path: `file:///google/src/cloud/gauravrawat/summarize_latest_chats/google3/hr_agentic_solution/brd_v2.md`\nTotal Lines: 173\nTotal Bytes: 17521\nShowing lines 1 to 173\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: # Business Requirements Document (BRD) - HR Agentic Solution (MVP 1 - Revised)\n2: \n3: ## Executive Summary\n4: \n5: The **HR Agentic Solution** is a secure, enterprise AI-driven virtual assistant designed to provide employees with immediate, conversational access to HR services. By orchestrating workflows across core enterprise systems (WorkWeek HCM, ServiceImmediately ITSM/HRSD) and retrieving answers from approved HR policies, the assistant automates routine inquiries and facilitates self-service transactions.\n6: \n7: To ensure enterprise governance and zero-trust security during the MVP 1 phase, the solution deploys an **API Gateway Mock Identity Assertion Layer** (signed JWT session claims) that binds conversational turns to authenticated employee profiles. The system enforces traceably bounded execution, authenticates automation origins for all backend operations, and dynamically validates interactions with probabilistic safety benchmarks to protect against prompt injection and data leaks.\n8: \n9: ---\n10: \n11: ## 1. Project Objectives\n12: \n13: * **Deflect Tier 1 Inquiries (40% Target):** Reduce routine HR and IT helpdesk ticket volume by at least 40% within the first six months.\n14:   * *Baseline Assumption:* 5,000 Tier-1 inquiries/month (60% IT Helpdesk, 40% HR Inquiries).\n15:   * *Deflection Definition:* A session is counted as deflected if the user resolves their inquiry via grounded policy Q&A (without opening a ticket within 24 hours) or successfully completes a self-service transaction without human escalation.\n16:   * *Target Volum\n<truncated 14745 bytes>\nsuccessful transaction reference ID. |\n151: \n152: ---\n153: \n154: ## 6. MVP 1 Implementation Architecture & Constraints\n155: \n156: * **Gateway Mock Identity Assertion Layer:** To satisfy zero-trust RBAC and origin verification without blocking on enterprise Okta/SSO integration, the Chat UI includes a test persona selector that issues signed JWT assertion headers (`X-Authenticated-User`) to the API Gateway. The Gateway verifies the signature, and the Agent Orchestrator enforces caller data boundaries.\n157: * **Backend Credentials:** Backend adapters connect to WorkWeek and ServiceImmediately sandbox instances using dedicated service accounts scoped by the API Gateway's asserted caller context.\n158: * **Tenancy Scope:** Deployed as a single-tenant instance for MVP 1 evaluation.\n159: \n160: ---\n161: \n162: ## 7. Success and Evaluation Criteria\n163: \n164: | Evaluation Category | Success Metric / Criterion | Target Benchmark |\n165: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n166: | **Deflection Rate** | Tier-1 tickets deflected via self-service and policy answers | 40% reduction (2,000 tickets/month deflected by Month 6) |\n167: | **Policy Q&A Quality** | Grounded precision, recall, and citation validity | &gt;= 95% Accuracy on test benchmark; 0% Hallucination |\n168: | **Transaction Integrity** | Correct execution of leave submissions and ticket operations | 100% Correctness; 0 duplicate transactions |\n169: | **Cross-System Chaining** | Successful execution of UC-2.1 and UC-2.2 with DLQ fallback | 100% pass on test suite scenarios |\n170: | **Safety & Guardrails** | Interception of jailbreaks, injections, and toxic prompts | &gt;= 99.5% Detection rate; &lt; 1% False Positive rate |\n171: | **Response Latency** | End-to-end turnaround across RAG and tool calls | Policy Q&A p95 &lt; 3.0s; Chained workflows p95 &lt; 7.0s |\n172: | **Audit Traceability** | Log coverage of all system actions and blocked requests | 100% Log Coverage with caller identity binding |\n173: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n","truncated_fields":["content"]}
{"step_index":280,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-17T16:14:38Z","content":"# Architectural Stress-Test & Gap Analysis: HR Agentic Solution BRD (MVP 1 - Revised)\n\n**Document Under Review:** `HR Agentic Solution BRD (MVP 1 - Revised & Approved)`  \n**Role:** Principal Product Manager & Principal Systems Architect  \n**Evaluation Phase:** Final Pre-Implementation Gate  \n\n---\n\n## Executive Summary\n\nThe **Revised BRD (MVP 1)** has successfully resolved the primary architectural contradictions present in the draft version (namely, the identity/SSO contradiction, uncalibrated deflection metrics, SAGA rollback ambiguity, and unit mismatches). \n\nHowever, before engineering begins Sprint 1, this stress-test identifies **secondary operational gaps, distributed state race conditions, and integration edge cases** that must be codified in technical designs to avoid runtime defects.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Clarity & Ambiguity Check\n\n```\n┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                              REVISED BRD AMBIGUITY AUDIT                               │\n├─────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┤\n│ Section & Item          │ Current Formulation           │ Engineering Ambiguity / Gap  │\n├─────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────┤\n│ FR-2.1: Disambiguation  │ \"Accurately parse intents...  │ ⚠️ No threshold for multi-   │\n│                         │ contextual disambiguation\"    │ intent queries (e.g., PTO\n<truncated 18530 bytes>\n\n  Scenario: User asks for policy rules and requests leave in a single turn\n    Given the employee has 10.0 days of Vacation balance\n    When the user prompts \"What is the bereavement policy, and also book vacation for next Friday?\"\n    Then the agent must retrieve the Bereavement policy and cite the official section\n    And the agent must calculate 1.0 work day for next Friday\n    And the response must contain:\n      | Section | Content |\n      | Top Text | Grounded Bereavement policy answer with citation deep link |\n      | Bottom Component | Interactive Vacation Confirmation Card for 1.0 work day |\n    And the leave transaction must remain pending until user confirms.\n```\n\n#### Scenario 4: DLQ Forward Recovery Event Schema Validation\n```gherkin\nFeature: Dead-Letter Queue Forward Resilience\n\n  Scenario: Verifying Cloud Pub/Sub DLQ payload structure on downstream failure\n    Given the employee confirmed a 5-day Medical Leave request\n    When WorkWeek records the Leave of Absence (Ref: \"LOA-8821\")\n    But ServiceImmediately returns HTTP 503 after 2 retries\n    Then the system must publish a Cloud Pub/Sub message to \"hr-ops-dead-letter-topic\"\n    And the published message must match the schema:\n      | Key | Expected Value |\n      | workflow_name | UC-2.2-MedicalLeave |\n      | primary_reference_id | LOA-8821 |\n      | employee_id | EMP-1042 |\n      | failed_step | ServiceImmediately_CreateITRoutingTicket |\n    And the user must receive a confirmation with WorkWeek Ref \"LOA-8821\" and HR Ops alert notice.\n```\n\n---\n\n## 7. Sign-Off & Implementation Readiness\n\n| Review Area | Architecture Status | Sign-Off Gate |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| **Identity & Auth Specification** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **GCP Cloud Run & Vertex AI Topology** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **API Data Contracts & Idempotency** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **Safety Guardrails & DLP Redaction** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |\n| **MoSCoW Backlog & Agile User Stories** | ✅ Complete | Ready for Sprint 1 |","truncated_fields":["content"]}
